<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Royal Prussia</h1><p class="st-meta">1466 CE – 1772 CE</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The western part of Prussia that rebelled against the Teutonic Knights and lived for three centuries under the Polish Crown, keeping its own diet and privileges.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1454 the towns and nobles of the Prussian Confederation rose against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order</a> and placed themselves under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV of Poland</a>. After the Thirteen Years' War, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-peace-of-thorn">Second Peace of Thorn</a> in 1466 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Chelmno Land</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malbork">Malbork</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elblag">Elblag</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-warmia">Prince-Bishopric of Warmia</a>, which together formed Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> kept wide autonomy, with its own diet, council and laws, and its great cities of Gdansk, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Torun</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elblag">Elblag</a> were largely German-speaking and Lutheran after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. Gdansk shipped Polish grain to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> and became the Commonwealth's richest city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a> was born in Torun in 1473. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a> in 1569 the province was drawn more closely into the Crown.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> in 1772 gave most of Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a>, which made it the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-prussia">West Prussia</a>, and Gdansk and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Torun</a> followed in 1793. Gdansk later became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-city-of-danzig-1807-1814">Free City of Danzig</a> (1807-1814).</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/royal-prussia.jpg" alt="Royal Prussia" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Лобачев Владимир · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Prussia_(1466-1772)_Lob.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1466 CE – 1772 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Casimir IV</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic and Lutheran Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Poland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Polish Prussia, Prussia Royal</dd></dl></aside></div>
